Rating guidelines: A company that actively welcomes and collaborates with labor unions, pays thriving living wages with comprehensive benefits, maintains exemplary workplace safety records, and provides humane scheduling and reasonable quotas gets a 100. A company that complies with minimum wage laws and basic OSHA standards, avoids egregious labor citations, but does not actively encourage collective bargaining gets a 50. A company that deploys aggressive union-busting firms, fires labor organizers, misclassifies workers as gig contractors, imposes grueling surveillance-driven quotas, or suffers frequent preventable workplace injuries gets a 1.
